module stdlib.math

# The Double (real-valued) math layer. The Int surface lives in `stdlib.math.int`;
# the two are split so each type gets the plain, unprefixed names in its own module
# (Sprout has no overloading, so one name cannot serve both). Import qualified when
# you need both:
#
#   import stdlib.math as math
#   import stdlib.math.int as imath
#   math.pow(t, 4.0)     # Double
#   imath.pow(2, 10)     # Maybe Int
#
# Partiality convention (docs/math-partiality-v0.md). Two domains, two rules:
#   Rule 1 — Int out-of-domain -> Maybe. Governs `stdlib.math.int` (mod, pow).
#   Rule 2 — Double out-of-domain -> IEEE-style NaN / +-inf. Governs THIS module
#            (sqrt, tan, ln, log2, log10, log, pow, cbrt). Terminal — it will not
#            migrate. Strict: no roundoff tolerance (a tiny-negative input is NaN,
#            not 0), and IEEE in spirit not bit-exact (this layer is pure-Sprout).
# A partial function follows exactly one; nothing returns a silent in-band lie.
# Callers detect a Rule-2 NaN with `is_nan`.

# --- Double primitives (M2 math layer) ---
# All pure Sprout, no C builtins. Slower than libm, which is fine for the transform-scale
# math this layer serves; if a measured hot path ever demands it, the right escalation is
# lowering to an LLVM intrinsic (e.g. llvm.sqrt.f64), not a runtime builtin.
#
# ACCURACY IS NOT UNIFORM ACROSS THIS BLOCK — do not size a tolerance from one figure:
#
#   sqrt, cbrt, exp, ln, log2, log10, log   ~1e-14 relative, whole exponent range
#   pow, integer exponent                   exact when every intermediate product is
#                                           (see `ipow`); ~1e-14 otherwise
#   pow, fractional exponent                ~1e-13 (it composes exp and ln, so it
#                                           inherits both errors)
#   sin, cos                                ~2e-8 absolute ONLY — the Taylor series here
#                                           are truncated for the transform-scale use they
#                                           were written for, six orders looser than the
#                                           rows above
#   tan                                     NO SINGLE FIGURE — see the warning below. It
#                                           is sin/cos, so cos's ~2e-8 ABSOLUTE error
#                                           becomes an unbounded RELATIVE one as cos -> 0
#   atan, atan2                             1.6e-11 absolute worst case over the whole
#                                           line, but 8e-16 on [-1, 1] — the large-argument
#                                           figure is set by the halving reduction
#                                           saturating, not by the series
#   asin, acos                              ~2e-15 absolute, ~5e-16 relative — TIGHTER than
#                                           the trig rows despite being built on `atan`,
#                                           because they only ever hand it an argument of
#                                           magnitude <= 1, where it is at its best
#
# THE `tan` WARNING, because a single number would be a lie. Accuracy degrades continuously
# as x approaches an odd multiple of pi/2 — this is not a point singularity at the pole.
# Measured relative error against libm, by distance from pi/2:
#
#   0.5 away    1.7e-9        5e-6 away   4.5e-3
#   5e-2 away   3.3e-7        5e-8 away   3.1e-1   (31% wrong)
#   5e-4 away   4.5e-5        5e-10 away  9.8e-1   (the answer is noise)
#
# So `tan` near a pole returns a large, plausible-looking, arbitrarily wrong number. Callers
# working near pi/2 must bound their distance from it, not merely avoid the pole exactly.
#
# Per-function measurements and method: docs/math-transcendental-v0.md.
#
# The LANGUAGE still has no Double -> Int conversion (only `to_double`, an `sitofp`);
# this module supplies one, `to_int`, in pure Sprout over the `double_to_bits`
# intrinsic. It returns `Maybe Int` because NaN, the infinities and |x| >= 2^63 have
# no Int to name. The rounding family — `floor`/`ceiling`/`truncate`/`round` — stays
# in Double and is total; compose it with `to_int` to pick a direction. A separate
# private `round_to_int` remains for the O(1) reductions, which need the binary
# exponent as an `Int` on a hot path and can prove their own inputs in range.
#
# The invariant the old "counters are Double-valued integers" note existed to justify
# still holds, and is what matters: EVERY REDUCTION MOVES ONLY BY EXACT POWERS OF TWO,
# so none of them contributes rounding error of its own. The stride ladders got that by
# multiplying by exact power-of-two constants; the bit-pattern forms get it by rewriting
# the exponent field while carrying the mantissa bits over untouched. Either way the
# residual is series truncation alone, which is what the accuracy figures above measure.

export let pi = 3.141592653589793

let two_pi = 6.283185307179586
let half_pi = 1.5707963267948966
# 2^52: at this magnitude a Double's ULP is exactly 1, so adding then subtracting
# it snaps the fractional bits away — the classic branch-free round-to-integer.
let two52 = 4503599627370496.0

# --- Exact power-of-two ladder ---
# Every range reduction below strips or applies powers of two. Moving one factor at a
# time costs O(|binary exponent|) steps — up to 1070 for a subnormal, which measured
# ~110x the small-argument cost (exp near x=688 was 1099 ns/call against 9.9 ns on
# [0,10)). These strides let each loop take the exponent apart in chunks, bounding it at
# ~20 steps instead. Every constant is an exact power of two whose reciprocal is also
# exact and finite (2^-512 is still a normal Double), so no step introduces rounding.
#
# The strides are grouped by what each consumer needs an exact root of: sqrt reduces by
# powers of FOUR (sqrt(4) = 2 is representable), cbrt by powers of EIGHT, exp and ln by
# any power of two.
fn sq(v: Double) -> Double = v * v

let two8 = 256.0
let two12 = 4096.0
let two16 = sq(two8)
let two32 = sq(two16)
let two48 = two32 * two16
# 2^54, exactly representable — so 1.0 / two54 is exact too. Written as a plain
# literal: Sprout has no exponent literals (`2e-17` is a parse error), but 2^54 has
# only 17 integer digits, well inside the 2^63 the lexer accepts. Used by exp's
# subnormal path and by the O(1) exponent decomposition, which scales a subnormal up
# by this before extracting its exponent.
let two54 = 18014398509481984.0
let two64 = sq(two32)
let two128 = sq(two64)
let two256 = sq(two128)
let two384 = two256 * two128
let two512 = sq(two256)

let inv_two8 = 1.0 / two8
let inv_two12 = 1.0 / two12
let inv_two16 = 1.0 / two16
let inv_two32 = 1.0 / two32
let inv_two48 = 1.0 / two48
let inv_two64 = 1.0 / two64
let inv_two128 = 1.0 / two128
let inv_two256 = 1.0 / two256
let inv_two384 = 1.0 / two384
let inv_two512 = 1.0 / two512

# --- O(1) exponent decomposition via IEEE bit access ---
#
# The reductions below split a positive Double into `mantissa * 2^exponent` in
# CONSTANT time, using `double_to_bits` / `double_from_bits` (compiler intrinsics
# that lower to nothing — see stdlib/prelude.sprout and
# docs/double-bit-access-v0.md). Before this, reduction walked the exponent in
# power-of-two strides: ~20 compare-multiply-branch steps, which is exactly why
# `ln(1e-300)` and `sqrt(1e300)` were 13-20x libm while normal magnitudes were 1-2x.
#
# NO BIT MASKS ARE NEEDED, and that is a precondition rather than a coincidence.
# For x > 0 the sign bit is 0, so the value is below 2^63 and integer division by
# 2^52 leaves exactly the 11 exponent bits. Every caller here guarantees x > 0:
# `sqrt` rejects negatives, `ln` rejects negatives and zero, `cbrt` takes the
# magnitude and re-applies the sign. Sprout has no bitwise operators at all — `>>`
# and `<<` are function composition — so this is what makes the technique available
# without adding any.
#
# The mantissa comes out by SUBTRACTION, not masking: forcing the exponent field to
# the bias yields m in [1, 2) with the mantissa bits untouched, so the split is
# exact and contributes zero rounding error — the same property the old
# power-of-two ladder had, and the reason accuracy is unchanged.

# One unit in the binary64 exponent field, and the exponent bias.
let exp_field_unit = 4503599627370496
let exp_bias = 1023

# `two54` (2^54) is defined further down for exp's subnormal path and reused here to
# lift a subnormal into the normal range, so one code path covers the whole domain.

# The raw biased exponent field of a positive finite x. O(1).
#
# TWO results are out-of-band and every caller must handle them:
#   0     — x is SUBNORMAL. The decomposition does not hold; each `*_reduce` below
#           lifts by 2^54 and threads an `adj` correction (see sqrt_reduce).
#   2047  — x is NaN or infinity. NOT a real exponent, though it decodes as one:
#           this is what made `sqrt(NaN)` return 1.64e154 before `sqrt` gained its
#           `is_nan` guard. Callers must reject non-finite inputs BEFORE reducing;
#           all four entry points now do.
fn raw_biased_exp(x: Double) -> Int =
  double_to_bits(x) / exp_field_unit

# 2^n as an exact Double, for n in the NORMAL exponent range [-1022, 1023]. Outside
# that the true value is subnormal or infinite and this formula silently produces
# garbage, so a caller that can reach further must split the scaling — exp_scale does.
fn two_pow(n: Int) -> Double =
  double_from_bits((n + exp_bias) * exp_field_unit)

# --- Floor-dividing the exponent, branchlessly ---
#
# The root reductions need floor(e/2) and floor(e/3), but Sprout's `/` truncates
# toward ZERO, which is not floor for a negative numerator: `-1 / 2` is 0 where
# floor(-0.5) is -1. The difference is not cosmetic — at x = 0.5 the exponent is -1,
# so floor gives j = -1 and a mantissa of 2.0 (inside [1,4)), while truncation gives
# j = 0 and a mantissa of 0.5, OUTSIDE the interval Newton is seeded for. Every
# negative odd exponent hits it: half of all inputs below 1.0.
#
# Rather than correct after the fact (`if a < 0 && q*n != a then q-1`, which needs a
# branch and, having no `%` operator, a multiply to test the remainder), the
# numerator is BIASED non-negative first. A binary64 exponent is never below -1074,
# and 1074 is divisible by both 2 and 3, so
#
#     floor(e/n)  ==  (e + 1074) / n  -  1074/n        for every e >= -1074
#
# because (e + 1074) is non-negative, where truncation and floor coincide, and the
# constant shift comes back out exactly. Verified exhaustively over the whole
# domain e in [-1074, 1023] for both divisors.
#
# This is not micro-optimisation for its own sake: the branchy version cost `cbrt`
# ~10% at normal magnitudes (7.41 vs 6.66 ns), which is exactly the "gave back at
# the common case what it won at the extremes" trap that `ln` hit earlier.
let exp_floor_bias = 1074

fn floor_div_2(e: Int) -> Int = (e + exp_floor_bias) / 2 - 537

fn floor_div_3(e: Int) -> Int = (e + exp_floor_bias) / 3 - 358

# A general `binary_exponent(x)` helper has now been written and deleted TWICE —
# once for `ln`, once for sqrt/cbrt/exp. Each reduction ends up inlining the
# decomposition in its own `where` block, because it already has the bit pattern in
# hand and wants ONE division rather than two, so the general helper never acquires
# a caller. A private function with no callers is still emitted into the IR of every
# module that imports this one. If you reach for it a third time, check whether your
# caller can inline it first.

# Divide a positive finite x by 2^n exactly, by subtracting n from its exponent
# field. Exact and rounding-free — the mantissa bits are carried over untouched.
# Requires the result to be normal, which every caller here guarantees by
# construction (they divide by the value's own exponent, landing in [1, 2^k)).
fn scale_down_pow2(x: Double, n: Int) -> Double =
  double_from_bits(double_to_bits(x) - n * exp_field_unit)

# --- Why the reductions thread an accumulator ---
# `sqrt_reduce`, `ln_reduce` and `cbrt_reduce` each carry the extracted factor along as a
# parameter and combine it with the series result at the base case. The tidier-looking
# alternative is to return the decomposition and let the caller put it together:
#
#   fn ln_split(x: Double, k: Double) -> (Double, Double) = ... else (x, k)
#   fn ln_reduce(x: Double) -> Double = k * ln2 + ln_series(m) where (m, k) = ln_split(x, 0.0)
#
# That was measured, not assumed. Both forms in one binary over the same 2M-call sweep give
# BIT-IDENTICAL results, and the tuple form costs 12.4 ns/call against 4.3 — 2.8x slower warm,
# and ~29.5 ns/call on a cold heap, the extra being GC. Re-run it with
# bench/math_transcendental/accumulator_vs_tuple_bench.sprout; numbers in
# bench/results-2026-08-06-math-transcendental.md.
#
# The cause is a missing case in tuple-return CPR (docs/scalar-replacement-v0.md), not
# anything inherent to tuples. CPR *does* fire on the outer call: a `where`-destructuring
# caller gets `{i64, i64}` returned by value, with no heap tuple at all. It does NOT fire on
# the SELF-RECURSIVE edge, so every reduction step round-trips through the boxed wrapper —
# `sprout_alloc_tuple_blob(16)`, two loads to unpack, an insertvalue pair to repack. At ~20
# steps per call that is ~20 allocations where the accumulator form has zero, and the
# self-tail-call that TCO would turn into a loop is lost as well. Both halves of this are
# pinned by `tests/stdlib/compiler/test_tuple_return_cpr.spr`.
#
# So this shape is deliberate. It is NOT a leftover workaround for the `where`-binding
# inference bug (bindings took their type from body usage instead of the right-hand side;
# fixed 2026-08-06). That bug did make the tuple form fail to compile while this code was
# first written, which is how it came to be recorded as the reason — but the form compiles
# fine now and is still the slower choice, so the accumulator stands on its own merits.
#
# Revisit only if the CPR gap closes; it is filed in BACKLOG.md under Sprout-IR / Model-C
# Codegen with these measurements.

# The IEEE quiet NaN — the out-of-domain Double result (Rule 2). Double `/` lowers
# to an unguarded fdiv (only integer `/` traps), so 0.0/0.0 is a genuine NaN.
export let nan = 0.0 / 0.0

# Detect a Rule-2 NaN: NaN is the only Double not equal to itself (ordered `==`).
export fn is_nan(x: Double) -> Bool = ! (x == x)

# The zero case is not redundant: `-0.0 < 0.0` is FALSE (IEEE compares the two zeros
# equal), so a plain `if x < 0.0` returned -0.0 unchanged and abs kept the sign it exists
# to remove. IEEE-754 abs, and C `fabs` whose name this now carries, clear the sign bit
# unconditionally. Returning the literal `0.0` normalises both zeros to +0.0; NaN falls
# through both tests and propagates.
export fn abs(x: Double) -> Double =
  if x < 0.0 then -x
  else if x == 0.0 then 0.0
  else x

# Round to nearest integer (ties to even) with no Int conversion. Only exact for
# |x| < 2^52; larger Doubles are already integral, so pass them through.
#
# The 2^52 magic-number round works ONLY for x >= 0: for a NEGATIVE x, (x + 2^52)
# drops below 2^52 into the ULP=0.5 binade, so it snaps to a half-integer instead
# of an integer (e.g. -0.5477 -> -0.5) and floor then computes -1.5. So round the
# magnitude and re-sign — round_nearest(x) = -round_nearest(-x) for x < 0.
fn round_nearest_pos(x: Double) -> Double =
  if x >= two52 then x
  else (x + two52) - two52

fn round_nearest(x: Double) -> Double =
  if x < 0.0 then 0.0 - round_nearest_pos(0.0 - x)
  else round_nearest_pos(x)

# The zero short-circuit is the mirror image of the one in `abs`: round_nearest sends
# -0.0 down its positive branch, so (-0.0 + 2^52) - 2^52 came back as +0.0. IEEE requires
# floor to preserve the sign of a zero argument, so pass both zeros through untouched.
export fn floor(x: Double) -> Double =
  if x == 0.0 then x
  else if r > x then r - 1.0
  else r
  where r = round_nearest(x)

# `floor` with the comparison flipped: step UP when round-to-nearest went down.
# The same zero short-circuit applies, and for the same reason.
export fn ceiling(x: Double) -> Double =
  if x == 0.0 then x
  else if r < x then r + 1.0
  else r
  where r = round_nearest(x)

# Toward zero — floor on the positive side, ceiling on the negative. The guard is
# `x < 0.0`, which is FALSE for -0.0, so -0.0 takes the floor branch and its sign
# survives.
export fn truncate(x: Double) -> Double =
  if x < 0.0 then ceiling(x) else floor(x)

# Nearest, TIES TO EVEN — inherited for free from the ties rule of IEEE addition,
# since round_nearest is the `(x + 2^52) - 2^52` trick and that add rounds ties to
# even. Matches Haskell's `round`; Rust's `round` differs, taking ties away from
# zero (its ties-to-even spelling is `round_ties_even`).
export fn round(x: Double) -> Double = round_nearest(x)

# --- Why the root iterations run a fixed pass count ---
# `sqrt_iter` and `cbrt_iter` below run an UNCONDITIONAL 6 Newton passes. Neither tests
# whether it has converged, which looks wasteful and is the opposite: the test cost more
# than the passes it saved.
#
# Both used to run up to 60 passes, exiting early on `abs(next - guess) < 1e-15 * guess`.
# Measured on a 2M-call sweep across the reduced interval, dropping that guard is
# **3.86x on sqrt** (7.17 -> 1.86 ns/call) and **2.50x on cbrt** (7.99 -> 3.20 ns/call).
#
# The cost was never the arithmetic — the inlined `fdiv` count is essentially unchanged
# (13 vs 11 for sqrt). It was the BRANCH. The guard makes the trip count depend on the
# input, so the loop-exit branch is unpredictable and mispredicts on nearly every call
# (~15-20 cycles, ~5 ns at 3.5 GHz). A fixed count makes it statically known, which also
# lets LLVM fully unroll: arm64 `-O2` emits `fcmp=0` and no per-pass branch at all, against
# `fcmp=2` / 8 conditional branches for the guarded form.
#
# 6 is not a guess. The guarded version took up to 7 passes; 6 unconditional passes match
# or beat its accuracy everywhere, and 5 does not — sqrt degrades to 9.3e-08 and cbrt to
# 3.7e-08, five orders outside this module's contract. Worst relative residual over 400k
# samples: sqrt 4.39e-16 across [1,4), cbrt 9.38e-16 across [1,8), both pinned by
# `tests/stdlib/test_math_root_accuracy.spr`.
#
# NOT bit-compatible for cbrt. `sqrt` is bit-identical to the guarded version at every one
# of 400k samples, because it had already converged before the guard fired. `cbrt` differs
# by ~1 ULP on about 3.5% of inputs: its guard fired at different pass counts for different
# inputs (3 near x=8, 7 elsewhere), so no fixed count can reproduce it. cbrt's worst-case
# residual IMPROVES, 1.005e-15 -> 9.38e-16, and callers comparing exact cbrt bit patterns
# against pre-2026-08-06 output will see a difference.
#
# The obvious alternative was measured and rejected: iterating the inverse cube root
# (`r' = r*(4 - x*r^3)/3`) is division-free, but the five serially-dependent multiplies form
# a LONGER latency chain than the single fdiv they replace — 5.41 ns/call against 3.20.
#
# Full write-up: bench/results-2026-08-06-math-transcendental.md.

# Newton-Raphson (Heron), quadratic convergence. Seeded from a value reduced into [1,4),
# so the seed is within a factor of two of the root (see sqrt_reduce).
fn sqrt_iter(x: Double, guess: Double, i: Int) -> Double =
  if i >= 6 then guess
  else sqrt_iter(x, 0.5 * (guess + x / guess), i + 1)

# Reduce the magnitude into [1, 4) by exact powers of four, tracking the square root of
# the extracted factor — a power of two, so also exact. Four is the right stride because
# sqrt(4) = 2 is representable, which is what keeps the scale-back error-free.
#
# REGRESSION GUARD. Newton used to be seeded with `x` itself. For a guess far above the
# root the Heron step only roughly HALVES it, so reaching the right order of magnitude
# alone took ~log2(x) iterations: above ~1e35 the 60-step ceiling was hit first and
# sqrt_iter returned its unconverged guess as the answer — sqrt(1e40) gave 8.674e21
# instead of 1e20, silently, which is exactly the in-band lie this module's header
# forbids. Reduced into [1,4) the seed is within a factor of two of the root, so Newton
# converges in a handful of steps for every representable input.
#
# `scale` is threaded rather than returned as `(mantissa, scale)` for a measured reason —
# see "Why the reductions thread an accumulator" above.
# O(1) since 2026-08-07 — the exponent comes out of the bit pattern rather than
# being walked in strides. `sqrt_reduce_strided` below is the retained oracle.
#
# sqrt(x) = sqrt(m) * 2^j where x = m * 2^(2j). The exponent must be split into an
# EVEN part (which halves exactly) and a remainder that stays with the mantissa, so
# j = floor(e/2) and m lands in [1, 4) — the interval sqrt_iter is seeded for.
#
# floor, not truncation: see floor_div_2 above. At x = 0.5 (e = -1) floor gives j = -1
# and m = 2.0; truncation would give j = 0 and m = 0.5, seeding Newton outside its
# interval. That is half of all inputs below 1.0, not a corner case.
#
# Both the division by 2^(2j) and the multiply by 2^j are exact powers of two, so
# the reduction contributes zero rounding error — the same property the stride
# ladder had, and why the two agree bit-for-bit.
fn sqrt_reduce(x: Double) -> Double =
  if raw_biased_exp(x) == 0 then sqrt_reduce_norm(x * two54, 0 - 54)
  else sqrt_reduce_norm(x, 0)

# `adj` is the exponent correction owed by a subnormal lifted by 2^54. It is folded
# into the exponent BEFORE the split, never applied to the result afterwards — the
# same single-rounding discipline ln_reduce_norm documents.
fn sqrt_reduce_norm(x: Double, adj: Int) -> Double =
  two_pow(j) * sqrt_iter(m, m, 0)
where
  e = raw_biased_exp(x) - exp_bias + adj
  j = floor_div_2(e)
  m = scale_down_pow2(x, 2 * j - adj)

# The pre-2026-08-07 stride ladder, retained as the accuracy oracle for the O(1)
# form (tests/stdlib/test_math_wide_reduction.spr cross-checks them). It is an
# independently-derived implementation of the same split, which is what makes it
# worth keeping — a test comparing a function against itself proves nothing. It
# earned that keep immediately: it is what showed `sqrt(NaN)` had regressed.
#
# THE COST, measured rather than hand-waved. Exporting the oracles means they are
# emitted into the IR of any module that imports `stdlib.math`, even under a
# selective import list — `tests/smoke_shapes/10_double_math.spr` imports exactly
# `(sqrt, cbrt, exp, ln, pow)` and still carries 1136 lines of oracle IR, ~7% of the
# file. Today that is the ONLY affected file in the tree (no shipped example imports
# `stdlib.math`; `astar` imports `stdlib.math.int`), so the present cost is close to
# nil, but it grows with every future consumer.
#
# Kept anyway, deliberately. Moving the ladders into the test file would mean
# duplicating `sqrt_iter`/`cbrt_iter`/`exp_series`/`ln_series` there too, since they
# are private — and that makes the oracle WORSE, not just noisier: a legitimate
# change to a series would then show up as a reduction divergence, which is a false
# alarm in exactly the test meant to be trusted. Sharing the series and varying only
# the reduction is the whole point.
#
# The real gap is that Sprout has no test-only visibility (no `#[cfg(test)]`, no
# friend modules), so "exported" is the only way a test can reach this. Filed in
# BACKLOG; when that lands, drop the `export` on the four `*_strided` entry points.
fn sqrt_reduce_strided(x: Double, scale: Double) -> Double =
  if x >= 4.0 then
    if x < two8 then sqrt_reduce_strided(x * 0.25, scale * 2.0)
    else if x < two64 then sqrt_reduce_strided(x * inv_two8, scale * 16.0)
    else if x < two512 then sqrt_reduce_strided(x * inv_two64, scale * two32)
    else sqrt_reduce_strided(x * inv_two512, scale * two256)
  else if x < 1.0 then
    if x >= inv_two8 then sqrt_reduce_strided(x * 4.0, scale * 0.5)
    else if x >= inv_two64 then sqrt_reduce_strided(x * two8, scale * 0.0625)
    else if x >= inv_two512 then sqrt_reduce_strided(x * two64, scale * inv_two32)
    else sqrt_reduce_strided(x * two512, scale * inv_two256)
  else scale * sqrt_iter(x, x, 0)

# Domain: x >= 0. A negative argument is outside the real domain -> NaN (Rule 2),
# not the old silent 0.0. Both zeros return x unchanged (preserving -0.0, per IEEE)
# and skip the Newton step, whose first guess would divide 0/0. +inf is its own
# root, but Newton would compute inf/inf = NaN, so short-circuit it — only +inf
# satisfies `x * 0.5 == x` among positive x.
# The `is_nan` guard is REQUIRED, not defensive, and its absence was a shipped bug.
#
# Under the old stride ladder `sqrt` needed no NaN guard and had none: every one of
# the ladder's comparisons (`x >= 4.0`, `x < 1.0`, …) is false against NaN, so NaN
# fell straight through to the base case and `sqrt_iter` propagated it. The O(1)
# reduction instead reads the EXPONENT FIELD, and NaN's field is 2047 — an entirely
# ordinary-looking exponent. It decoded NaN as 1.5 * 2^1024 and returned
# 1.6421143998800672e+154, and pushed e = 1024 into floor_div_2, outside the
# [-1074, 1023] domain that helper is verified for.
#
# `ln`, `exp` and `cbrt` all already guarded; `sqrt` was the only one that did not,
# precisely because it was the only one that had never needed to.
export fn sqrt(x: Double) -> Double =
  if is_nan(x) then x
  else if x < 0.0 then nan
  else if x == 0.0 then x
  else if x * 0.5 == x then x
  else sqrt_reduce(x)

# sqrt via the reference stride ladder. Exported for the cross-check test only —
# the production path is `sqrt`.
export fn sqrt_strided(x: Double) -> Double =
  if x < 0.0 then nan
  else if x == 0.0 then x
  else if x * 0.5 == x then x
  else sqrt_reduce_strided(x, 1.0)

# --- Exponential, logarithm, cube root, power ---
# Each is range reduction into a narrow interval + a series (or Newton) there. The
# reductions all move by exact powers of two, so they contribute no rounding error;
# the residual error is the series truncation alone. Measured bounds are in
# docs/math-transcendental-v0.md.

let ln2 = 0.6931471805599453
let ln10 = 2.302585092994046
# 1/ln2 — lets exp's reduction multiply instead of divide.
let inv_ln2 = 1.4426950408889634
let sqrt2 = 1.4142135623730951
let inv_sqrt2 = 0.7071067811865476
# The positive IEEE infinity. Double `/` lowers to an unguarded fdiv (only integer
# `/` traps), so this is a genuine +inf rather than a trap, exactly like `nan` above.
let inf = 1.0 / 0.0
# IEEE negative zero. Written with unary `-` (which lowers to fneg, flipping the sign bit);
# `0.0 - 0.0` would give +0.0. Needed as a return value where C99 distinguishes the zeros.
let neg_zero = -0.0
# `two54` is defined with the other powers of two near the top of the file — it is
# needed by the O(1) exponent decomposition, which appears above this point, and a
# module-level `let` must be defined before use.

# exp on the reduced |r| <= ln2/2, as Horner-nested Taylor terms through r^12
# (truncation ~1e-16 at the worst case). Nested as acc_k = 1 + r*acc_(k+1)/k so the
# factorial denominators stay visible — no table of 1/k! decimal constants to mistype.
#
# Written `r * (1.0 / k)` rather than `r / k` deliberately. `1.0 / k` is literal over
# literal, so LLVM folds it to a constant and each term costs one multiply. The `r / k`
# form divides a runtime value, and clang can only rewrite the divisors that are exact
# powers of two (2, 4, 8) — the arm64 -O2 output keeps 8 real fdiv instructions where
# this form has none. Measured worth ~20% of the series cost, about 0.8ns per exp call
# (bench/results-2026-08-06-math-transcendental.md); the earlier guess that it would be
# several times faster did not survive measurement. The extra rounding from an inexact
# reciprocal is invisible: max relative error is 7.98e-14 with either form.
fn exp_series(r: Double) -> Double =
  1.0 + r * (1.0 + r * (1.0 / 2.0) * (1.0 + r * (1.0 / 3.0) * (1.0 + r * (1.0 / 4.0)
    * (1.0 + r * (1.0 / 5.0) * (1.0 + r * (1.0 / 6.0) * (1.0 + r * (1.0 / 7.0)
    * (1.0 + r * (1.0 / 8.0) * (1.0 + r * (1.0 / 9.0) * (1.0 + r * (1.0 / 10.0)
    * (1.0 + r * (1.0 / 11.0) * (1.0 + r * (1.0 / 12.0))))))))))))

# Multiply y by 2^k for a Double-valued integer k. Every step is an exact binary scaling,
# so while the result stays normal this is error-free.
#
# Strides descend 512 -> 64 -> 8 -> 1 so the step count is O(log |k|): ~20 rather than the
# 1076 a one-at-a-time loop needed, which is what made exp cost 1099 ns/call near x=688
# against 9.9 ns on small arguments. Safe against intermediate underflow because y shrinks
# monotonically toward its final value, so if the result is normal every intermediate was
# too — and exp's caller routes the subnormal case through the two-step path below.
# The coarse strides are NESTED behind the `|k| >= 1` tests rather than listed ahead of
# them. A flat chain that checks 512/64/8 first costs the common small-k caller four extra
# comparisons and four module-global loads on every call — measured as a 2.6x slowdown on
# ln's hot path when this was first written flat. Nested, the common path pays two
# comparisons and never touches the ladder constants.
# --- Round-to-nearest AND Double -> Int, in one add (the magic-number trick) ---
#
# Sprout has no Double -> Int conversion (`to_double` only goes the other way), but
# exp's scale factor has to exist as BOTH: as a Double for the reduction
# `r = x - k*ln2`, and as an Int for `two_pow` to build 2^k.
#
# One operation serves both. At magnitude 2^52 a Double's ULP is exactly 1, so
# `v + 2^52` snaps the fractional bits away — that is the branch-free round-to-integer
# `round_nearest` already used. The integer it snaps to also lands in the MANTISSA
# field, so subtracting the bit pattern of 2^52 reads it straight back out as an Int.
# Rounding and conversion are the same add.
#
# An earlier version did them separately (`round_nearest` then a distinct
# `int_of_integral`), adding and subtracting 2^52 twice on exp's hot path for what is
# one operation. The Double side is now recovered with `to_double`, a bare sitofp.
#
# Domain: |v| < 2^52. exp's k is bounded by its own guards to [-1076, 1025], so the
# only caller is far inside it.
#
# Stays PRIVATE now that `to_int` exists. It answers a narrower question than the
# public conversion does — round-to-nearest only, |v| < 2^52 only, total — and it
# is on exp's hot path, so it must not acquire the guards `to_int` needs.
#
# Bits of 2^52: exponent field 52+1023 = 1075, mantissa 0 -> 1075 * 2^52.
# `two52` itself is defined with the other powers of two near the top of the file.
let two52_bits = 4841369599423283200

fn round_to_int_pos(v: Double) -> Int =
  double_to_bits(v + two52) - two52_bits

fn round_to_int(v: Double) -> Int =
  if v < 0.0 then 0 - round_to_int_pos(0.0 - v)
  else round_to_int_pos(v)

# --- Double -> Int, the public conversion ---
#
# The layer splits in two and the split is the point. `floor`/`ceiling`/`truncate`/
# `round` are TOTAL because they stay in Double: NaN and the infinities pass
# through and no range question arises. `to_int` is the ONE partial function, so
# it is the one place that decides what NaN, the infinities and |x| >= 2^63 mean.
# A caller composes them — `to_int(floor(x))` floors, `to_int(ceiling(x))` rounds
# up — and `to_int` alone truncates toward zero, as Rust, Go and C do, so it parts
# company with `to_int(floor(x))` only on negative non-integral input.
#
# `Maybe Int` rather than a saturating `Int`, matching `parse_int`: a conversion
# that cannot answer says so instead of returning a plausible wrong number.
#
# 2^63, as a bound rather than a literal — the lexer's integer range stops one
# short of it, and it is not a value any Int can hold.
let two63 = two64 / 2.0

# Int's minimum, spelled without a literal for the same reason.
fn min_int() -> Int = 0 - 9223372036854775807 - 1

# An already-integral magnitude in [0, 2^63) to an Int, exactly.
#
# `round_to_int_pos` alone cannot do this: it adds 2^52, which for m >= 2^52 has
# no mantissa room left and returns the wrong answer. Splitting m into a high and
# a low half at 2^32 puts BOTH halves under 2^52, where the magic-constant trick
# is exact. Every step is exact in Double: dividing by 2^32 only shifts the
# exponent, and the residual is a difference of integers below 2^32.
fn magnitude_to_int(m: Double) -> Int =
  hi * 4294967296 + round_to_int_pos(lo)
where
  hi_d = floor(m / two32)
  hi = round_to_int_pos(hi_d)
  lo = m - hi_d * two32

# NaN is tested FIRST and cannot be folded into the range guard: every comparison
# against NaN is false, so `t >= two63 || t < -two63` is false for a NaN and would
# fall through to convert garbage.
#
# The range boundary is ASYMMETRIC. Int's maximum, 2^63 - 1, is not representable
# as a Double — the spacing at that magnitude is 1024, so the neighbouring Doubles
# are 2^63 - 1024 and 2^63 itself. Int's minimum, -2^63, is a power of two and IS
# exact, so it converts. It also cannot go through the negate-and-re-sign path,
# whose positive half would be 2^63, so it is answered directly.
export fn to_int(x: Double) -> Maybe Int =
  if is_nan(x) then Nothing
  else if t >= two63 then Nothing
  else if t < 0.0 - two63 then Nothing
  else if t == 0.0 - two63 then Just(min_int())
  else if t < 0.0 then Just(0 - magnitude_to_int(0.0 - t))
  else Just(magnitude_to_int(t))
where
  t = truncate(x)

# `to_int` with the fallback written at the call site, mirroring `vec_get_or`.
export fn to_int_or(x: Double, fallback: Int) -> Int =
  match to_int(x) with
  | Just n -> n
  | Nothing -> fallback

# O(1) since 2026-08-07: build 2^k directly instead of walking k in strides.
#
# Range. exp's guards bound x to [-746, 710], so k = round(x/ln2) lands in
# [-1076, 1025], and exp's own `k < -1021` branch shifts the subnormal tail up by
# 54 before calling here — so `ki` arrives in [-1022, 1025]. `two_pow` is exact on
# [-1022, 1023]; past 1023 the exponent field would overflow into the sign bit and
# silently yield a NEGATIVE number, so ki > 1023 is split into two exact factors.
#
# Splitting rather than short-circuiting to inf, because k = 1024 does NOT mean the
# result overflows: y is exp_series(r) with |r| <= ln2/2, so y is in [0.707, 1.415],
# and y * 2^1024 is finite whenever y <= 1 - 2^-53 — i.e. just under 1.0. (DBL_MAX is
# (2 - 2^-52) * 2^1023, so the bound is (2 - 2^-52)/2. An earlier draft of this
# comment said "~1.34", which is wrong and dangerous in this specific spot: it is the
# stated justification for the split, so anyone trusting it could widen the guard and
# reintroduce the very overflow bug the split exists to prevent.)
# exp(709.78) is exactly that case —
# k = 1024 with a finite result of ~1.79e308, just under DBL_MAX. A first version
# returned inf there and `test_math_transcendental`'s "exp(709.78) is finite"
# caught it; the wide-reduction sweep did NOT, because its 0.37 step from -745
# stopped at 698 and never reached the overflow boundary. The sweep now runs past
# 710 as well.
#
# y * 2^1023 cannot itself overflow (1.415 * 8.99e307 = 1.27e308 < DBL_MAX), so the
# split is safe, and the trailing multiply overflows to +inf exactly when it should.
#
# One multiply means ONE rounding, where the stride ladder rounded at every step.
fn exp_scale(y: Double, ki: Int) -> Double =
  if ki > 1023 then y * two_pow(1023) * two_pow(ki - 1023)
  else y * two_pow(ki)

# The pre-2026-08-07 stride ladder, retained as the accuracy oracle.
fn exp_scale_strided(y: Double, k: Double) -> Double =
  if k >= 1.0 then
    if k < 8.0 then exp_scale_strided(y * 2.0, k - 1.0)
    else if k < 64.0 then exp_scale_strided(y * two8, k - 8.0)
    else if k < 512.0 then exp_scale_strided(y * two64, k - 64.0)
    else exp_scale_strided(y * two512, k - 512.0)
  else if k <= -1.0 then
    if k > -8.0 then exp_scale_strided(y * 0.5, k + 1.0)
    else if k > -64.0 then exp_scale_strided(y * inv_two8, k + 8.0)
    else if k > -512.0 then exp_scale_strided(y * inv_two64, k + 64.0)
    else exp_scale_strided(y * inv_two512, k + 512.0)
  else y

# exp(x) = 2^k * exp(r) where x = k*ln2 + r and |r| <= ln2/2.
#
# The overflow/underflow guards are not just IEEE etiquette — they are what makes
# exp_scale terminate. Without them a +-inf argument yields k = +-inf and the scaling
# loop would spin forever, since inf-1.0 == inf.
#
# Below k = -1021 the result is subnormal, and halving one step at a time re-rounds a
# subnormal on every step: 54 successive round-to-even cascades flush exp(-745) to 0.0
# instead of ~5e-324. Scaling to 54 places above the target and dividing by 2^54 once
# collapses those 54 roundings into one.
# `ki` is produced by ONE rounding step, not two. The scale factor is needed both as
# an Int (to build 2^k) and as a Double (for the reduction `r = x - k*ln2`), and an
# earlier version got each from its own add-and-subtract of 2^52 — the same operation
# performed twice. `round_to_int` does the rounding and the conversion in one add, and
# `to_double` recovers the Double side with a bare sitofp.
export fn exp(x: Double) -> Double =
  if is_nan(x) then x
  else if x > 710.0 then inf
  else if x < -746.0 then 0.0
  else if ki < -1021 then exp_scale(exp_series(r), ki + 54) / two54
  else exp_scale(exp_series(r), ki)
where
  ki = round_to_int(x * inv_ln2)
  k = to_double(ki)
  r = x - k * ln2

# exp via the reference stride ladder. Exported for the cross-check test only —
# the production path is `exp`.
export fn exp_strided(x: Double) -> Double =
  if is_nan(x) then x
  else if x > 710.0 then inf
  else if x < -746.0 then 0.0
  else if k < -1021.0 then exp_scale_strided(exp_series(r), k + 54.0) / two54
  else exp_scale_strided(exp_series(r), k)
where
  k = round_nearest(x * inv_ln2)
  r = x - k * ln2

# ln on the reduced m in [1/sqrt2, sqrt2) via the atanh series: with
# s = (m-1)/(m+1), ln(m) = 2*(s + s^3/3 + s^5/5 + ...), Horner-nested on s^2 through
# s^15. Centering the reduction on sqrt2 instead of [1,2) caps |s| at 0.172 rather
# than 1/3, which halves the terms needed for the same accuracy.
fn ln_series(m: Double) -> Double =
  2.0 * s * (1.0 + s2 * (1.0 / 3.0 + s2 * (1.0 / 5.0 + s2 * (1.0 / 7.0 + s2
    * (1.0 / 9.0 + s2 * (1.0 / 11.0 + s2 * (1.0 / 13.0 + s2 * (1.0 / 15.0))))))))
where
  s = (m - 1.0) / (m + 1.0)
  s2 = s * s

# Halve or double into [1/sqrt2, sqrt2), COUNTING the powers of two rather than
# accumulating ln2 per step, then multiplying once at the end.
#
# The count matters for accuracy, not just speed. Adding ln2 on every step accumulated one
# rounding per step — 1070 of them for a subnormal — giving 1.7e-11 absolute error at
# ln(2^-1070) where the code claimed ~1e-13, and making floor(log2(x)) return the wrong
# integer for roughly half of all exact powers of two. `k` here is an exact integer (every
# integer up to 2^53 is representable), so `k * ln2` is a single rounding regardless of how
# far the reduction travelled — strictly more accurate AND ~1070 fewer operations.
#
# Strides descend 512 -> 64 -> 8 -> 1, as in exp_scale, bounding the loop at ~20 steps.
#
# `k` is threaded rather than returned as `(mantissa, k)` for a measured reason — see "Why
# the reductions thread an accumulator" above. This function is the one the 4.25 vs 12.5
# ns/call comparison there was taken on.
# O(1) since 2026-08-07: the exponent comes straight out of the bit pattern instead
# of being walked in strides. The stride ladder this replaces is preserved in
# `ln_reduce_strided` below, which the accuracy tests cross-check against.
#
# The series wants its argument in [1/sqrt2, sqrt2), but the decomposition yields
# [1, 2). One conditional halving covers the gap: for m >= sqrt2, use m/2 and
# carry the extra power of two in k. Halving is exact, so this costs no accuracy.
#
# k stays a Double and is combined as `k * ln2` in ONE multiply, exactly as the
# strided version did — every integer up to 2^53 is exactly representable, so a
# single rounding regardless of how large the exponent is. See the comment above on
# why the decomposition is not returned as a tuple.
# The decomposition is written out inline rather than factored into general
# exponent/mantissa helpers: each such helper performs its own signed division, and
# this is the hot path where one division is worth having. `b` and `biased` are
# bound once and shared by both halves. (Those helpers were written and deleted
# twice — see the note above raw_biased_exp's callers.)
#
# Subnormals recurse ONCE through the 2^54 lift, using ln(x) = ln(x*2^54) - 54*ln2.
fn ln_reduce(x: Double) -> Double =
  if raw_biased_exp(x) == 0 then ln_reduce_norm(x * two54, 0.0 - 54.0)
  else ln_reduce_norm(x, 0.0)

# `adj` is the exponent correction owed by a subnormal that was lifted by 2^54.
#
# It is threaded in and folded into `k` BEFORE the multiply, rather than the caller
# subtracting `54.0 * ln2` afterwards. That is not a stylistic choice — the first
# version did subtract afterwards and `tests/stdlib/test_math_wide_reduction.spr`
# caught it: 5 subnormal samples disagreed with the stride-ladder oracle, because
# `54.0 * ln2` is one rounding and the subtraction a second, where the ladder does a
# single `k * ln2`. `k + adj` is exact (both are small integers, and every integer
# up to 2^53 is representable), so this restores the single-rounding property and
# the two forms agree bit-for-bit again.
fn ln_reduce_norm(x: Double, adj: Double) -> Double =
  if m >= sqrt2 then (k + adj + 1.0) * ln2 + ln_series(m * 0.5)
  else (k + adj) * ln2 + ln_series(m)
where
  b = double_to_bits(x)
  biased = b / exp_field_unit
  k = to_double(biased - exp_bias)
  m = double_from_bits(b - (biased - exp_bias) * exp_field_unit)

# The pre-2026-08-07 stride ladder, kept as the accuracy reference for the O(1)
# form above (tests/stdlib/test_math_wide_reduction.spr cross-checks them). Not
# dead code in the "forgot to delete it" sense: it is an independent
# implementation of the same mathematical split, arrived at differently, which is
# what makes it worth something as an oracle. Strides descend 512 -> 64 -> 8 -> 1.
fn ln_reduce_strided(x: Double, k: Double) -> Double =
  if x >= sqrt2 then
    if x < two8 then ln_reduce_strided(x * 0.5, k + 1.0)
    else if x < two64 then ln_reduce_strided(x * inv_two8, k + 8.0)
    else if x < two512 then ln_reduce_strided(x * inv_two64, k + 64.0)
    else ln_reduce_strided(x * inv_two512, k + 512.0)
  else if x < inv_sqrt2 then
    if x >= inv_two8 then ln_reduce_strided(x * 2.0, k - 1.0)
    else if x >= inv_two64 then ln_reduce_strided(x * two8, k - 8.0)
    else if x >= inv_two512 then ln_reduce_strided(x * two64, k - 64.0)
    else ln_reduce_strided(x * two512, k - 512.0)
  else k * ln2 + ln_series(x)

# ln via the reference stride ladder. Exported for the cross-check test only — the
# production path is `ln`.
export fn ln_strided(x: Double) -> Double =
  if is_nan(x) then x
  else if x < 0.0 then nan
  else if x == 0.0 then 0.0 - inf
  else if x * 0.5 == x then x
  else ln_reduce_strided(x, 0.0)

# Domain: x >= 0 (Rule 2 — a negative argument is NaN, not a clamped 0). ln(0) is the
# pole at -inf. +inf must be short-circuited or the halving loop would never exit;
# among positive x only +inf satisfies `x * 0.5 == x`, the same test sqrt uses.
export fn ln(x: Double) -> Double =
  if is_nan(x) then x
  else if x < 0.0 then nan
  else if x == 0.0 then 0.0 - inf
  else if x * 0.5 == x then x
  else ln_reduce(x)

# Every logarithm is ln scaled by a constant. NaN and +-inf propagate from ln, so the
# Rule-2 domain behaviour is inherited rather than re-implemented three times.
export fn log2(x: Double) -> Double =
  ln(x) / ln2

export fn log10(x: Double) -> Double =
  ln(x) / ln10

# General base, argument first to match the `log2`/`log10` shape: log(8.0, 2.0) == 3.0.
export fn log(x: Double, base: Double) -> Double =
  ln(x) / ln(base)

# Newton for the cube root: g' = (2g + x/g^2)/3. Quadratically convergent, and from a
# guess in the right octave it takes at most 7 passes, so the 60-step ceiling only
# bounds pathological inputs. `* (1.0 / 3.0)` is a folded constant multiply rather than
# a division, for the reason given on exp_series; x / (guess * guess) divides by a
# runtime value and stays a real division — which is why cbrt remains the slowest row in
# the benchmark at ~22ns: 7 passes each carrying an unavoidable division.
#
# A linear initial guess (1 + (m-1)/7 across the reduced [1,8)) was measured and
# rejected: it cuts the worst case from 7 passes to 6, which does not pay for the code.
# Newton on g^3 = x, seeded at 2.0 from a value reduced into [1,8) (see cbrt_reduce).
# Fixed 6 passes; rationale and measurements on sqrt_iter above.
fn cbrt_iter(x: Double, guess: Double, i: Int) -> Double =
  if i >= 6 then guess
  else cbrt_iter(x, (2.0 * guess + x / (guess * guess)) * (1.0 / 3.0), i + 1)

# Reduce the magnitude into [1, 8) by exact factors of 8, tracking the cube root of the
# extracted factor — a power of two, so also exact. 8 is the right stride because
# cbrt(8) == 2 is representable; that is what keeps the scale-back error-free.
#
# The coarse strides are the powers of two divisible by three (2^384, 2^48, 2^12, 2^3), so
# each has an exact cube root (2^128, 2^16, 2^4, 2). Same motive as exp_scale and
# ln_reduce: one-factor-at-a-time took 357 steps at 1e-300 and measured 392 ns/call.
#
# `scale` is threaded rather than returned as `(mantissa, scale)` for a measured reason —
# see "Why the reductions thread an accumulator" above.
# O(1) since 2026-08-07, same shape as sqrt_reduce but splitting the exponent into
# thirds: cbrt(x) = cbrt(m) * 2^j where x = m * 2^(3j), j = floor(e/3), m in [1, 8)
# — the interval cbrt_iter's seed of 2.0 is chosen for.
#
# floor again, not truncation, and the mod-3 case is stricter than sqrt's mod-2:
# TWO of every three negative exponents disagree between floor and truncation
# rather than one of two.
fn cbrt_reduce(x: Double) -> Double =
  if raw_biased_exp(x) == 0 then cbrt_reduce_norm(x * two54, 0 - 54)
  else cbrt_reduce_norm(x, 0)

fn cbrt_reduce_norm(x: Double, adj: Int) -> Double =
  two_pow(j) * cbrt_iter(m, 2.0, 0)
where
  e = raw_biased_exp(x) - exp_bias + adj
  j = floor_div_3(e)
  m = scale_down_pow2(x, 3 * j - adj)

# The pre-2026-08-07 stride ladder, retained as the accuracy oracle. See
# sqrt_reduce_strided for why an independently-derived implementation is kept.
fn cbrt_reduce_strided(x: Double, scale: Double) -> Double =
  if x >= 8.0 then
    if x < two12 then cbrt_reduce_strided(x * 0.125, scale * 2.0)
    else if x < two48 then cbrt_reduce_strided(x * inv_two12, scale * 16.0)
    else if x < two384 then cbrt_reduce_strided(x * inv_two48, scale * two16)
    else cbrt_reduce_strided(x * inv_two384, scale * two128)
  else if x < 1.0 then
    if x >= inv_two12 then cbrt_reduce_strided(x * 8.0, scale * 0.5)
    else if x >= inv_two48 then cbrt_reduce_strided(x * two12, scale * 0.0625)
    else if x >= inv_two384 then cbrt_reduce_strided(x * two48, scale * inv_two16)
    else cbrt_reduce_strided(x * two384, scale * inv_two128)
  else scale * cbrt_iter(x, 2.0, 0)

# cbrt is defined on the WHOLE real line — unlike sqrt, a negative argument is in
# domain (cbrt(-8) == -2), so it is odd symmetry, not Rule 2. Both zeros pass through
# with their sign; +-inf is its own cube root.
#
# Deliberately not exp(ln(x)/3): the octave reduction above is exact, so cbrt neither
# inherits exp/ln's truncation error nor depends on them.
export fn cbrt(x: Double) -> Double =
  if is_nan(x) then x
  else if x == 0.0 then x
  else if x < 0.0 then 0.0 - cbrt(0.0 - x)
  else if x * 0.5 == x then x
  else cbrt_reduce(x)

# cbrt via the reference stride ladder. Exported for the cross-check test only.
export fn cbrt_strided(x: Double) -> Double =
  if is_nan(x) then x
  else if x == 0.0 then x
  else if x < 0.0 then 0.0 - cbrt_strided(0.0 - x)
  else if x * 0.5 == x then x
  else cbrt_reduce_strided(x, 1.0)

# Is y an odd integer? Computed entirely in Double (no fptosi exists): floor(y*0.5)
# doubles back to y exactly when y is even. Correct for negative y too — y = -3 gives
# floor(-1.5) = -2 and -3 - 2*(-2) == 1. For |y| >= 2^53 every Double is an even
# integer, and this reports even, which is the truth.
fn is_odd_int(y: Double) -> Bool =
  (floor(y) == y) && (y - 2.0 * floor(y * 0.5) == 1.0)

# An integer exponent small enough to be worth the exact path below.
fn is_small_int(y: Double) -> Bool =
  (floor(y) == y) && (abs(y) <= 1024.0)

# Binary exponentiation for a positive Double-valued integer exponent — multiplications
# only, no exp/ln round trip, so it is markedly more accurate than the general path and
# carries none of its truncation error.
#
# It is NOT unconditionally bit-exact, and an earlier version of this comment claimed it
# was. Binary exponentiation computes pow(T,4) as (T*T)*(T*T) — two roundings — whereas
# left-to-right T*T*T*T is ((T*T)*T)*T — three. They agree only when no intermediate
# product needs rounding: they do agree for T=5772 (5772^4 is exactly representable) and
# disagree for T=6726.387968863355, where a correctly-rounded libm pow differs from both.
# The honest contract is: exact when every intermediate product is exactly representable,
# otherwise within about an ulp — never a promise of equality with any particular
# multiplication order.
fn ipow(base: Double, e: Double, acc: Double) -> Double =
  if e < 1.0 then acc
  else if e - 2.0 * h == 1.0 then ipow(base * base, h, acc * base)
  else ipow(base * base, h, acc)
where
  h = floor(e * 0.5)

# x^(-e) for a positive integer e. NOT simply 1.0 / ipow(x, e): x^e can overflow to
# +inf while x^-e is perfectly representable — 2^1024 overflows, so the reciprocal
# collapses to 0.0 where the true 2^-1024 is 5.56e-309. When x^e overflows, recompute as
# (1/x)^e, which cannot overflow for e in this range. That reciprocal is inexact for a
# base that is not a power of two, but an inexact answer beats a wrong zero, and this
# path is only reached when the direct route has already failed.
fn ipow_recip(x: Double, e: Double) -> Double =
  if p * 0.5 == p then ipow(1.0 / x, e, 1.0)
  else 1.0 / p
where
  p = ipow(x, e, 1.0)

# pow(x, y). The edge cases follow C99/IEEE (F.9.4.4), which differs from Python:
# pow(0.0, -1.0) is +inf here, where Python raises. Ordered so the two total cases win
# before any NaN test, because C99 makes them total even for a NaN operand:
#   pow(x, +-0) == 1 for every x, NaN included
#   pow(1, y)   == 1 for every y, NaN included
# A negative base is in domain only for an integer exponent (otherwise the result is
# genuinely not real -> NaN, Rule 2); the sign then follows the exponent's parity.
export fn pow(x: Double, y: Double) -> Double =
  if y == 0.0 then 1.0
  else if x == 1.0 then 1.0
  else if is_nan(x) || is_nan(y) then nan
  # INFINITE EXPONENT. Only |x| against 1 decides the result; the base's SIGN is
  # irrelevant, because an infinite exponent is even in the limit. pow(-1, +-inf) is 1
  # (C99 makes it total, since |-1| is exactly 1 and neither limit dominates).
  else if y * 0.5 == y then
    if abs(x) == 1.0 then 1.0
    else if (abs(x) > 1.0) == (y > 0.0) then inf
    else 0.0
  else if x == 0.0 then
    # The pole. IEEE keeps the sign of a -0.0 base under an odd integer exponent, so
    # returning `x` for y > 0 propagates -0.0, and the y < 0 branch gives -inf.
    if y > 0.0 then
      if is_odd_int(y) then x
      else 0.0
    else if is_odd_int(y) && (1.0 / x < 0.0) then -inf
    else inf
  # INFINITE BASE, finite nonzero exponent. Tested after the x == 0.0 arm above, because
  # `x * 0.5 == x` is also true of both zeros. The magnitude saturates, so only the
  # exponent's sign picks inf vs 0; a negative base additionally carries its sign through
  # an odd integer exponent. This arm is why the negative-base NaN rule below is correct
  # only for a FINITE base: C99 F.9.4.4 confines that rule to finite x < 0, and routing
  # -inf into it returned NaN where the standard requires +-inf or +-0.
  else if x * 0.5 == x then
    if y > 0.0 then
      if (x < 0.0) && is_odd_int(y) then -inf
      else inf
    else if (x < 0.0) && is_odd_int(y) then neg_zero
    else 0.0
  else if x < 0.0 then
    if floor(y) == y then
      # Negate with unary `-`, which lowers to fneg and flips the sign bit. `0.0 - v`
      # looks equivalent but is not: when v is +0.0 (an underflowed magnitude) it yields
      # +0.0, losing the sign that C99 requires for an odd negative exponent.
      if is_odd_int(y) then -pow(-x, y)
      else pow(-x, y)
    else nan
  else if is_small_int(y) then
    if y > 0.0 then ipow(x, y, 1.0)
    else ipow_recip(x, 0.0 - y)
  else exp(y * ln(x))

# Reduce an angle into [-pi, pi] so the Taylor series stays accurate.
fn reduce(x: Double) -> Double =
  x - two_pi * floor((x + pi) / two_pi)

# sin on a reduced argument via Horner-nested Taylor terms through x^17
# (truncation error ~2e-8 at the |r|=pi worst case). Denominators are (2k)(2k+1).
fn sin_series(r: Double) -> Double =
  r * (1.0 - rr / 6.0 * (1.0 - rr / 20.0 * (1.0 - rr / 42.0 * (1.0 - rr / 72.0
    * (1.0 - rr / 110.0 * (1.0 - rr / 156.0 * (1.0 - rr / 210.0 * (1.0 - rr / 272.0))))))))
  where rr = r * r

export fn sin(x: Double) -> Double =
  sin_series(reduce(x))

export fn cos(x: Double) -> Double =
  sin(x + half_pi)

export fn radians(deg: Double) -> Double =
  deg * pi / 180.0

# tan = sin/cos. Undefined (division by IEEE 0 -> inf) at odd multiples of pi/2 — but the
# pole is the least of it, and guarding only against the exact pole is not enough. cos
# carries ~2e-8 of ABSOLUTE error, so as cos(x) -> 0 that becomes an unbounded RELATIVE
# error in the quotient: 4.5e-5 at 5e-4 from pi/2, 0.31 at 5e-8, 0.98 at 5e-10. The result
# stays large and finite and plausible the whole way down, so nothing signals the loss.
# Callers near pi/2 must bound their DISTANCE from it; see the tan warning in the header.
export fn tan(x: Double) -> Double =
  sin(x) / cos(x)

# atan via the Maclaurin series, accurate only for small |x|: sum (-1)^k x^(2k+1)/(2k+1)
# through x^13, Horner-nested on x2 = x*x. The reduction below keeps |x| small before this runs.
fn atan_series(x: Double) -> Double =
  x * (1.0 - x2 * (1.0 / 3.0 - x2 * (1.0 / 5.0 - x2 * (1.0 / 7.0 - x2
    * (1.0 / 9.0 - x2 * (1.0 / 11.0 - x2 * (1.0 / 13.0)))))))
  where x2 = x * x

# Halving reduction: atan(x) = 2*atan(x / (1 + sqrt(1 + x^2))), applied `depth` times before the
# series. Each pass shrinks the argument toward 0 (and SATURATES — even huge x collapses to ~1 on the
# first pass, ~0.2 by the third), so a fixed depth of 3 covers the whole real line. Measured
# worst absolute error 1.6e-11, and that figure is set entirely by the saturation: an argument
# of magnitude <= 1 reduces to 0.098 where the series is good to 8e-16, while a huge one only
# reaches 0.199 where the x^13 truncation is ~2e-12, doubled three times back up to 1.6e-11.
fn atan_reduce(x: Double, depth: Int) -> Double =
  if depth <= 0 then atan_series(x)
  else 2.0 * atan_reduce(x / (1.0 + sqrt(1.0 + x * x)), depth - 1)

export fn atan(x: Double) -> Double =
  atan_reduce(x, 3)

# Full-circle bearing atan2(y, x) in (-pi, pi]. atan alone loses the sign of x (a target dead-ahead
# and dead-astern share a ratio), so the x<0 branch adds ±pi to place the quadrant, and x==0 handles
# the poles. atan2(0,0) is defined as 0 (there is no meaningful direction; 0 is the conventional pick).
export fn atan2(y: Double, x: Double) -> Double =
  if x > 0.0 then atan(y / x)
  else if x < 0.0 then
    if y >= 0.0 then atan(y / x) + pi
    else atan(y / x) - pi
  else if y > 0.0 then half_pi
  else if y < 0.0 then -half_pi
  else 0.0

# asin via the half-angle identity asin(x) = 2*atan(x / (1 + sqrt(1 - x^2))), which follows
# from tan(t/2) = sin(t) / (1 + cos(t)). The textbook form atan(x / sqrt(1 - x^2)) is unusable
# over this atan: at |x| = 1 it feeds +-inf into atan_reduce, whose x / (1 + sqrt(1 + x*x))
# step then evaluates inf/inf = NaN. The half-angle denominator never drops below 1, so the
# endpoints land on the finite 2*atan(+-1) and no argument ever leaves atan's accurate range.
#
# Out of domain (|x| > 1) the radicand goes negative, sqrt yields NaN and NaN propagates —
# Rule 2, docs/math-partiality-v0.md. Writing this as atan2(x, sqrt(1 - x*x)) instead would
# LOSE that: atan2 compares its NaN x against 0.0 twice (both false) and falls through to the
# `y > 0.0` arm, silently answering pi/2 for an out-of-domain input.
#
# The endpoints are answered directly rather than computed, because the series result there
# OVERSHOOTS the range: 2*atan(1) lands on 1.5707963267948974, one ulp above pi/2, which makes
# acos(1.0) a NEGATIVE -8.9e-16 and breaks the `acos(dot(u, v)) >= 0.0` that every
# angle-between-unit-vectors caller assumes. The overshoot is confined to exactly +-1: one ulp
# inward the true acos is already 1.5e-8 (it leaves 1 like sqrt(2*eps)), far above the error
# floor, so these two equalities restore the range guarantee across the whole domain. Verified
# by a 2M-sample sweep of [-1, 1] against libm: zero out-of-range results, worst absolute
# error 1.6e-15 (asin) and 1.8e-15 (acos). `test_math_double.spr` keeps a 20001-sample
# in-repo version of that range check, which fails if this branch is removed.
export fn asin(x: Double) -> Double =
  if x == 1.0 then half_pi
  else if x == -1.0 then -half_pi
  else 2.0 * atan(x / (1.0 + sqrt(1.0 - x * x)))

# acos in two arms, split at 0.5 — and the split point is forced, not tuned.
#
# The complement `half_pi - asin(x)` is correct everywhere but loses RELATIVE accuracy as
# x -> 1: there asin(x) -> pi/2, so the subtraction cancels two nearly-equal numbers and
# asin's ~1.6e-15 absolute error lands on a result of ~2e-8. Measured, that is a relative
# error of 2.8e-8 at x = 1 - 2^-52 — eight significant digits gone, on the arm every
# angle-between-unit-vectors caller uses most.
#
# The half-angle arm has no cancellation at all, because `1.0 - x` is EXACT: Sterbenz's
# lemma makes a - b exact whenever b/2 <= a <= 2b, which holds for every x in [0.5, 2].
# That is why the threshold is 0.5 — it is the Sterbenz boundary, so below it the identity
# would start rounding and buy nothing, and above it the subtraction is free of error.
# Halving is exact too (a power of two), so sqrt receives an exactly-representable argument.
#
# Measured over x = 1 - 2^-k for k = 1..52: worst relative error 2.8e-8 -> 4.7e-16. Nothing
# else moves — whole-domain worst absolute error stays 1.8e-15 with zero out-of-range
# results, and the exact values survive: acos(1.0) is +0.0 (now from 2*asin(sqrt(0.0)),
# independent of asin's endpoint branch), acos(-1.0) is pi, acos(0.0) is pi/2.
#
# Rule 2 survives both arms. NaN and any x > 1 fail `x > 0.5` into the complement arm only
# when NaN (NaN > 0.5 is false) and reach asin, which is NaN-propagating; a real x > 1 takes
# the half-angle arm, where 1.0 - x is negative and sqrt yields NaN.
export fn acos(x: Double) -> Double =
  if x > 0.5 then 2.0 * asin(sqrt((1.0 - x) * 0.5))
  else half_pi - asin(x)

# --- General Double utilities (clamping / interpolation) ---
# The two domain-agnostic numeric atoms every animation/graphics caller reaches for.
# clamp is the Double counterpart of `stdlib.math.int.clamp` — same name, different
# module, since the two modules exist precisely so neither name needs a marker prefix.
# lerp is the affine blend of a..b by t; t is UNclamped, so it also extrapolates
# (t<0 or t>1) — callers wanting a bounded blend clamp t first.

export fn clamp(x: Double, lo: Double, hi: Double) -> Double =
  if x < lo then lo
  else if x > hi then hi
  else x

export fn lerp(a: Double, b: Double, t: Double) -> Double =
  a + (b - a) * t
